    <h1>Awards<br />
    </h1>
  </div>

    The HAMER organizing committee will award two prizes for the best research presentations at 
    the event: one for the best oral presentation, and one for the best poster presentation.
    <br><br>
    Each prize will cover round-trip airfare for the award recipient to visit an academic or 
    research institution in Greece! These awards are sponsored by Leta Serafim in honor of the 
    memory of her husband Philip Serafim, professor at Northeastern University and National Technical University of Athens.
    <br><br>
    Please refer below for the evaluation criteria used for each presentation format.

    <hr style="border-color: #d3d3d3;" />


  <h2>Evaluation Criteria for Oral Presentations</h2>
  <p>The criteria by which the oral presentations will be evaluated are:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Delivery</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Is the presentation delivered smoothly, with a clear message that is retained by experts and non-experts in the audience?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Organization</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Does the presentation have a logical organization?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Visual Aids</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Are slides (text, plots, and other media) used well in conveying information?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Mastery of Subject</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Does the presenter demonstrate good knowledge of the subject?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Results</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Has the author presented the results clearly and in sufficient detail?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Innovation</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Has the author presented any innovative results?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Time</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Has the speaker stayed within the 12-minute limit for the presentation, leaving 3 minutes for audience questions?</em>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <hr style="border-color: #d3d3d3;" />

  <h2>Evaluation Criteria for Poster Presentations</h2>
  <p>The criteria by which the poster presentations will be evaluated are:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Delivery</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Is the presentation delivered smoothly, with a clear message that is retained by experts and non-experts in the audience?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Organization</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Does the presentation have a logical organization?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Visual Aids</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Did the poster have a good balance of text and graphics?<br>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Were the contents of the poster able to convey the overall message by themselves?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Mastery of Subject</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Does the presenter demonstrate good knowledge of the subject?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Results</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Has the author presented the results clearly and in sufficient detail?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Innovation</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Has the author presented any innovative results?</em>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Space &amp; Time</strong><br>
      <em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Was the speaker's use of the poster space optimal (informative but not crowded)?<br>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Was the speaker's topic explanation effective and succinct?</em>
    </li>
  </ol>

